= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume that each target's incorporated flux is proportional to its applied <power> over this operating range, and that the same composition convention is used throughout. The calibration gives $F_A/F_B=0.35/0.65$ at equal <powers>. Holding the A target unchanged while making the two incorporated fluxes equal therefore requires
$$
\boxed{P_B=30\frac{0.35}{0.65}=16.15\,\mathrm W}.
$$
The B <power> decreases because B originally supplies more material. This linear <magnetron sputtering> approximation neglects power-dependent yield, target condition, resputtering and changes in sticking probability. A second composition measurement is the experimental check.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the intended additive-deposition model, A originally contributes $0.35\times2.2=0.77\,\mathrm{nm\,s^{-1}}$. At equal incorporated contributions, B also supplies $0.77\,\mathrm{nm\,s^{-1}}$, giving **$1.54\,\mathrm{nm\,s^{-1}}$**. Equivalently, the new total incorporated flux is $0.35+0.35=0.70$ times the calibrated total.

There is a composition-convention qualification. If the percentages represent <mass> flux and the <mass density> stays fixed, the preceding thickness calculation follows directly. If they instead represent atomic fractions, a constant atomic number density would justify the same result, whereas constant <mass density> alone does not. With atomic <masses> $M_A,M_B$ and constant <mass density>, the ratio would be
$$
\frac{R_{\rm new}}{R_{\rm old}}=\frac{0.35(M_A+M_B)}{0.35M_A+0.65M_B}.
$$
The PDF does not specify atomic versus <mass> percentages or the two elements. Thus **1.54 nm/s is the intended common flux-to-thickness approximation**, and the formula above states the additional dependence if atomic fraction and constant <mass density> are meant literally.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Approximate each source as localized, keep the angular factors unchanged and use inverse-square flux dilution. The increased distance gives
$$
R_{120}\simeq R_{80}\left(\frac{80}{120}\right)^2=1.54\frac49=\boxed{0.684\,\mathrm{nm\,s^{-1}}}.
$$
This carries the composition/thickness convention of the previous answer. A greater source distance reduces fractional distance and angle variation across a given substrate, improving composition uniformity at the cost of rate. A real <magnetron sputtering> target is extended, with an angular emission distribution, and gas scattering can change the distance law; this is an estimate rather than a calibrated prediction.

